---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import "../../styles/docs.css";
---

<Base
	title="Loan payoff | Actual Budget Tweaks"
	description="See when a loan or line of credit is paid off and what it costs, in Actual Budget Tweaks."
>
	<main class="docs center">
		<header class="docs__header stack stack--sm">
			<a href="/guides" class="docs__back">Back to guides</a>
			<h1>Loan payoff</h1>
			<p class="docs__lead">
				Loan payoff works out when a loan, mortgage or line of credit is paid off and how much
				interest it costs on the way. Actual keeps the balance; you tell the panel the rate and
				the payment.
			</p>
		</header>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Setting up a loan</h2>
			<p>Turn on Loan payoff in the Features section of Tweaks, then:</p>
			<ol>
				<li>Open an account that owes money and click <strong>Payoff</strong> in its toolbar.</li>
				<li>Enter the annual rate and your monthly payment.</li>
				<li>
					Pick how interest is charged: <strong>Daily</strong> on each day's balance, as most
					lines of credit do, or <strong>Monthly</strong> as a twelfth of the rate, as most fixed
					loans do.
				</li>
				<li>Enter the days of the month the interest posts and the payment lands.</li>
				<li>Click <strong>Save and see payoff</strong>.</li>
			</ol>
			<p>
				Once saved, the account shows when it will be paid off beside its balance, and the panel
				opens on the payoff month, the interest still to come and a chart of the balance down to
				zero. Both follow the balance as transactions are added. The details fold away into
				<strong>Loan settings</strong>, where you can change or remove them.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Trying out changes</h2>
			<p>
				<strong>Try a change</strong> runs the saved plan with something different, and shows
				beside the payoff month how much sooner it finishes and what it does to the interest:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Payment</strong> slides the monthly payment up or down.</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Finish by</strong> takes a month and sets the payment that clears the debt by
					then.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Extra payments</strong> adds one-off payments. They are for trying out and
					aren't saved.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Nothing changes the saved plan until you click <strong>Use this plan</strong>, which saves
				the payment you tried. <strong>Reset</strong> goes back to the plan.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Checking the interest</h2>
			<p>
				Enter the payee your lender's interest charges are posted under as the
				<strong>Interest payee</strong> in Loan settings. The panel then uses those charges in two
				ways:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					It works out the rate you were actually charged from recent postings and warns under
					Loan settings when it has moved away from the rate you entered. Variable rates change
					without notice, and this is where it shows.
				</li>
				<li>
					Under the chart it shows the charge to expect on the next interest day. If that day has
					passed and the charge isn't in the account, <strong>Add</strong> enters it as an
					uncleared transaction, which you can undo from the notification.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Where the settings are kept</h2>
			<p>The settings are saved as one line in the account's note:</p>
			<pre><code>loan: rate=0.0514 daycount=act365 interestDay=20 payment=1860 paymentDay=23 interestPayee=RBC</code></pre>
			<p>
				The rest of the note is left as it is, and scripts using Actual's API can read the same
				line. <strong>Remove loan details</strong> in Loan settings takes the line back out.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>What it assumes</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>One rate and one payment from today onward, and no new borrowing.</li>
				<li>The payment is the same every month, not a share of the balance.</li>
				<li>Lenders round and count days in their own ways, so expect small differences.</li>
			</ul>
		</section>
	</main>
</Base>
